Implement a clean, efficient solution and don't over-engineer the problem. 
If you need to explain your approach, you may write it below. You may also need to explain the analysis of time or space complexity. 

You can assume that all inputs will always be valid.
But your function f should be effective for all inputs in constraints.
```
private static int getNumbers(int length, int offset, int start) {
        int result = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
            result += start + offset * i * i;
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static List<Integer> f(List<List<Integer>> inputs) {
        List<Integer> results = new ArrayList<>();
        for (List<Integer> input: inputs) {
            int N = input.get(0);
            int D = input.get(1);
            int X = input.get(2);
            int result = getNumbers(N, D, X);
            results.add(result);
        }
        return results;
    }
```

